00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.280
Like let's scrap it all and start again.

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:19.880
So I think there's something really interesting and especially with the aid of things like

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:25.241
review tools built in the loop, you know, no better time question mark to be someone

00:33:25.241 --> 00:33:27.120
who's writing code to do data science.

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:28.120
Yeah.

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:32.280
And I think there's a lot of hype around AI engineering and things like that.

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:35.160
And we internally at Hex have an AI engineering team.

00:33:35.481 --> 00:33:40.321
One thing that I've observed is sort of the lines between our AI engineering team and

00:33:40.321 --> 00:33:44.120
our data team really blurring and starting to overlap.

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:49.400
And so, for example, a lot of things that we'll do internally for our ourselves to build

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:52.840
our own product and build our own agent, we'll build, you know, we have evals, we have various

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:55.120
other things that we've built.

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:58.400
You know, we will look at those and be like, oh, we should actually take those and put

00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:02.440
those into the product for our customers.

00:34:02.440 --> 00:34:08.241
Because these workflows of, you know, building a highly trusted agent actually, you know,

00:34:08.241 --> 00:34:09.241
converge over time.

00:34:09.241 --> 00:34:10.241
Yeah.

00:34:10.241 --> 00:34:14.280
Who runs Hex's internal context studio?

00:34:14.280 --> 00:34:16.761
Who's the person curating that?

00:34:16.761 --> 00:34:17.761
Yeah.

00:34:17.761 --> 00:34:18.761
That's our data team.

00:34:18.761 --> 00:34:19.761
Nice.

00:34:19.761 --> 00:34:20.761
Yeah.

00:34:20.761 --> 00:34:21.761
And they are like our number one dog fooders.

00:34:21.761 --> 00:34:22.761
Yeah.

00:34:22.761 --> 00:34:23.761
For obvious reasons.

00:34:23.761 --> 00:34:24.761
Yeah.

00:34:24.761 --> 00:34:25.761
Do you see like the lines blurring elsewhere?

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:30.840
Not just because, I guess like in our, like we're starting to see like designers who previously

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:35.160
would create, you know, like a beautiful thing and turn it over to engineers to implement.

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:40.120
Now they can give a work, deliver a working prototype or like product people who previous

00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:42.160
were like, hey engineer, can you implement this?

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:44.000
They just go away and do that.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:47.840
Like are you seeing that blurring of lines in your engineering work too?

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:51.801
I would say absolutely.

00:34:51.801 --> 00:34:55.601
But I think it's a little bit less dramatic at Hex than maybe at other places because

00:34:55.601 --> 00:34:59.521
our org has always kind of been designed like this, if that makes sense.

00:34:59.521 --> 00:35:06.081
Most of our PMs used to be technical, like actually hands-on technical in some capacity.

00:35:06.081 --> 00:35:09.400
All of our designers, we like give them an interview that's designed to be like, can

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:12.400
you do light, you know, CSS and React work?

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:18.081
Like we don't want people to just be completely boxed out of a core part of the technology.

00:35:18.081 --> 00:35:21.521
You know, our front end engineers and our backend engineers kind of flow back and forth.

00:35:22.241 --> 00:35:25.801
Everybody has their spikes, you know, it's not like we're expecting, you know, a backend

00:35:25.801 --> 00:35:27.921
engineer to be, you know, deep in React.

00:35:27.921 --> 00:35:31.160
But we don't want people to have these really hard boundaries because that can often create

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:36.041
just like a lot of really annoying inefficiencies in the organization.

00:35:36.041 --> 00:35:37.560
And so, yes, we are seeing that blur.

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:40.761
We're sort of seeing that intensify, I would say, like, you know, designers and product

00:35:40.761 --> 00:35:45.960
managers are like, can go much deeper kind of in what they're able to contribute than

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:46.960
they used to be.

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:49.160
But, you know, we hired a lot of people who are excited about that.

00:35:49.160 --> 00:35:51.361
So I think that's like just strictly a good thing.

00:35:52.201 --> 00:35:53.201
Yeah, it's interesting.

00:35:53.201 --> 00:36:00.761
And maybe this is a nice segue into a less kind of AI, more style topic.

00:36:00.761 --> 00:36:03.880
But Hex has incredible style.

00:36:03.880 --> 00:36:04.880
Hex is just cool.

00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:08.361
I don't know how to explain, like, you go to the Hex website, and it's been like this

00:36:08.361 --> 00:36:12.960
for years and years and years, I feel like.

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:16.000
The website, cool.

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:19.960
The booths at conferences, unhinged.

00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:20.960
In a good way.

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:21.960
In a good way.

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:22.960
I'm sorry.

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:25.921
Like a retro dining booth.

00:36:25.921 --> 00:36:29.280
Hex is throwing like magic shows.

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:30.560
I don't know how to ask this.

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:33.440
How did Hex acquire such immaculate style?

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:37.641
Well, I'm going to have to give some credit to some other people in the organization for

00:36:37.641 --> 00:36:38.641
that one.

00:36:38.641 --> 00:36:43.840
My co-founder, Barry, the second person we ever hired was Adam, our head of design.

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:47.720
And I think, you know, those two have been kind of the masterminds.

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:51.880
So Annie, our head of brand design, have been the masterminds of a lot of these things.

00:36:51.880 --> 00:36:55.761
So in some ways, it's down to specific individuals and having particular individuals who have

00:36:55.761 --> 00:36:59.560
these kind of off-the-wall takes.

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:03.960
But I think also it's about the culture that you build as an organization and who you find

00:37:03.960 --> 00:37:09.640
and us trying to bring in people who are creative, who don't take themselves too seriously.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:14.840
And when you have some of those things, it creates an environment where you can be generative

00:37:14.840 --> 00:37:18.080
and think differently like this.

00:37:18.080 --> 00:37:20.361
And yeah, so it's both.

00:37:20.361 --> 00:37:23.801
There's like some very, very talented people that we have and also, you know, the team

00:37:23.801 --> 00:37:25.280
that we built around them.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:26.280
Yeah.

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:27.280
I really like the Hex.

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:31.840
Like the Hex color scheme is like so, like it really stands out and like tick.

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:34.041
No one is like pink and cream and brown.

00:37:34.041 --> 00:37:39.720
Like that's, I mean, probably now that's the next trend, but like, you know, I kind of

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:43.840
love, like we spent a lot of effort at Posit coming up with our logo and stuff, but just

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:49.840
like blue and orange is just like such a like traditional kind of tech color scheme.

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:50.840
It's yeah.

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:57.640
It's just fascinating to see like as we grow as a company, how these kind of forces of

00:37:57.640 --> 00:38:05.160
like unification and kind of corporatization, like pull on you towards these, just like

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:08.561
this is like the safe, safe choice.

00:38:08.561 --> 00:38:13.700
And yeah, and some of that just has to do with, you have to scale your content and branding

00:38:14.561 --> 00:38:15.561
engine.

00:38:15.561 --> 00:38:22.460
And so early on, a lot of creativity can actually just come from particular individuals.

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:26.941
And you almost always get certainly a more like unique results when you're, when you're

00:38:26.941 --> 00:38:29.780
doing that and you're doing that in small teams.

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:33.501
And then you get to a point where it's like, oh, you have to write all of this content

00:38:33.501 --> 00:38:37.580
and you have to put out things on Twitter and LinkedIn to get the, you know, SEO or

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:40.501
AEO or whatever we're doing now.

00:38:40.501 --> 00:38:45.140
And you just, the amount of stuff that you have to produce can't bottleneck through your

00:38:45.140 --> 00:38:48.061
main creative engines.

00:38:48.061 --> 00:38:53.301
And sometimes you can scale, like you obviously scale your brand voice and your brand styling

00:38:53.301 --> 00:38:57.140
and things like that, but you can't do it perfectly.

00:38:57.140 --> 00:39:00.260
And so I do think that, you know, we're, we're trying to fight really hard against this and

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:05.601
trying to like balance the two, but you do sometimes sort of inevitably have feel this

00:39:05.601 --> 00:39:09.381
like regression towards the mean pole, just like the more and more stuff you have to do

00:39:09.381 --> 00:39:10.381
on the branding side.

00:39:10.381 --> 00:39:11.381
Yeah.

00:39:11.381 --> 00:39:14.660
And it's sort of also interesting just as the company grows, like the, the amount of

00:39:14.660 --> 00:39:19.460
visibility you have within all of the parts of the organization.

00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:24.301
And this like sounds weird coming from a data person, but like you are forced to start developing

00:39:24.301 --> 00:39:30.280
like metrics so that you can get some oversight and then like metrics are good, obviously,

00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:34.760
but they're also bad because they kind of like trap you into these like predefined,

00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:38.981
like how, like how do you like wrestle with that as like clearly a data driven company,

00:39:38.981 --> 00:39:42.660
but you don't want to be like too data driven at the same time.

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:43.660
Yeah.

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:50.820
I think for me, a lot of it, a lot of it has come down to being able to sort of shift the

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:58.981
altitude at which you operate and then knowing when is the right time to be at what altitude.

00:39:58.981 --> 00:40:03.780
So obviously it's extremely important, you know, as a senior leader of a company to be

00:40:03.780 --> 00:40:05.981
thinking about strategy and long-term time horizons.

00:40:05.981 --> 00:40:08.820
And I spend a lot of time, obviously we spend a lot of time talking about AI.

00:40:09.660 --> 00:40:13.580
Our AI strategy is where I spend a lot of my time thinking.

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:18.820
And then, you know, you're intermediated through layers and layers of organization.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:23.660
And sometimes there's something down at the bottom that's like not going well.

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:29.020
And if you've built a good organization, you'll know about that most of the time, but sometimes

00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:30.020
you don't.

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:33.580
And sometimes you just, you see things in the data or you see things, you know, that

00:40:33.580 --> 00:40:35.900
don't feel right.

00:40:35.981 --> 00:40:43.460
And I think for me, cultivating that kind of like instinct of, hey, this section feels

00:40:43.460 --> 00:40:45.981
like it feels like there's something not working here.

00:40:45.981 --> 00:40:48.061
The vibes are off.

00:40:48.061 --> 00:40:51.301
And being able to just do a deep dive on that.

00:40:51.301 --> 00:40:56.381
And in order to be able to do that, you have to know enough about the things that are like

00:40:56.381 --> 00:40:57.381
happening on the ground.

00:40:57.381 --> 00:41:02.260
And so for me, you know, when I first started really building up a bunch of our AI programs,

00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:05.700
I spent, and I still do actually, like I read the papers, like I spent a bunch of time

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:10.340
in the technology and that's to both to like be able to see the big picture, but also when

00:41:10.340 --> 00:41:12.981
something's not right on the ground, to be able to kind of go all the way down and like

00:41:12.981 --> 00:41:16.700
help debug, you know, where something is breaking down.

00:41:16.700 --> 00:41:17.700
Yeah.

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:22.140
As CTO of Hex, I'm so curious, what is your, what does that look like?

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:25.421
Like what's your day to day look like?

00:41:25.421 --> 00:41:28.061
It's a pretty big range of things.

00:41:28.061 --> 00:41:33.541
I think one of the funny things about being CTO is sometimes I feel like my job is more

00:41:33.541 --> 00:41:37.660
about humans than it is about technology, even though technology is in the title.

00:41:37.660 --> 00:41:43.220
And so a lot of it is about what I'm not sort of thinking about the AI side of the AI technology

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:44.220
side of things.

00:41:44.220 --> 00:41:50.260
A lot of it is about like, how, how are we running a functional technology organization?

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:53.301
And there's engineering, there's data, there's, there's our survey, our research side, there's

00:41:53.301 --> 00:41:56.861
security, there's all of these things involved in that.

00:41:56.861 --> 00:42:01.900
And a lot of that day to day is actually not necessarily like making technology decisions.

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:05.301
Like I've hired smart people, if I've done my, especially in areas like security where

00:42:05.301 --> 00:42:09.220
I'm not the expert, if I've done my job right, I've hired people who are better at making

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:11.941
those technology decisions than I am.

00:42:11.941 --> 00:42:16.181
It's about, you know, setting up the humans to do their best work.

00:42:16.181 --> 00:42:20.460
But as someone who's like come up from the kind of technologic, like the technical side,

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:24.541
like how do you, like, do you still, is there some amount of programming like you just want

00:42:24.541 --> 00:42:27.621
to do to kind of like scratch that internal itch of like being?

00:42:27.621 --> 00:42:28.621
Let me cook.

00:42:28.621 --> 00:42:29.621
Yeah.

00:42:29.621 --> 00:42:31.220
Yeah, for sure.

00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:36.501
A lot of it I try to do on sort of side, less, you know, mission critical, critical path

00:42:36.501 --> 00:42:37.501
things right now.

00:42:37.501 --> 00:42:39.660
Some of it is actually fun to get.

00:42:39.660 --> 00:42:44.981
So when I was in college, my concentration was, was on called ML.

00:42:44.981 --> 00:42:47.220
And then I did my master's in NLP.

00:42:47.220 --> 00:42:51.621
And so I've always really been interested in this side of the house, but this was over

00:42:51.621 --> 00:42:52.621
10 years ago.

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:56.981
For people who might not know, NLP is natural language processing.

00:42:56.981 --> 00:42:58.541
And it's all about.

00:42:58.541 --> 00:43:04.340
It's basically all of, it's like pre LLMs, pre generative AI, how you operated with natural

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:10.340
language in, you know, in a statistical sort of modeling sense.

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:16.140
And actually a lot of the techniques back when I was in college were sort of hilariously

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:18.780
primitive versions of what we have today, if that makes sense.

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:21.741
So, but, you know, it was, it was pretty clear, this was 2012, it was pretty clear

00:43:21.741 --> 00:43:26.660
that this was like the stuff that they were doing was like kind of neat and you could

00:43:26.660 --> 00:43:29.941
like see some interesting properties of language with it.

00:43:29.941 --> 00:43:33.220
But like your ability, you couldn't generate text, like your, your ability to actually

00:43:33.220 --> 00:43:36.941
do things like machine translation was like a big area of study back then.

00:43:36.941 --> 00:43:40.640
And it was like, you know, comically bad, you know, it was like, you could get the gist

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:47.381
of what the, what the text was saying, but it wasn't anywhere approximating real speech.

00:43:47.381 --> 00:43:50.381
And it was pretty clear to me that like the techniques that we were learning about as

00:43:50.381 --> 00:43:55.020
state-of-the-art at the time were like, not it.

00:43:55.020 --> 00:43:58.460
And so I kind of like left that and went into just core software engineering because I was

00:43:58.460 --> 00:43:59.460
like, I want to do something real.

00:43:59.460 --> 00:44:01.121
I want to do something that's like practical.

00:44:01.121 --> 00:44:05.260
And then sure enough, a few years later, Attention Was All You Need was published and it's kind

00:44:05.260 --> 00:44:06.660
of like off to the races from there.

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:09.541
But so, so, so sometimes it's me getting hands-on and building things.

00:44:09.541 --> 00:44:13.280
And sometimes it's me just like getting back into that has been really fun and spending

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:16.941
some time, not just like reading blogs, but like reading the actual underlying papers

00:44:17.501 --> 00:44:21.381
and like understanding, understanding the research side has been, has been like super

00:44:21.381 --> 00:44:22.381
fun over the last couple of years.

00:44:22.381 --> 00:44:23.381
Yeah.

00:44:23.381 --> 00:44:24.381
Oh, that's interesting.

00:44:24.381 --> 00:44:25.381
So you've been kind of diving back into NLP.

00:44:25.381 --> 00:44:26.381
It kind of like reignited.

00:44:26.381 --> 00:44:27.381
Yeah.

00:44:27.381 --> 00:44:30.181
I mean, well now, now it's just, you know, all generative AI and it's like, it's sort

00:44:30.181 --> 00:44:31.181
of a blurred line.

00:44:31.181 --> 00:44:34.460
Large language models do so many more things other than just NLP.

00:44:34.460 --> 00:44:40.541
But yeah, a lot of, a lot of it is like, again, at the foundational level, like, you know,

00:44:40.541 --> 00:44:41.541
similar techniques.

00:44:41.541 --> 00:44:43.900
It's all just matrix multiplication under the hood anyway.

00:44:43.900 --> 00:44:44.900
It is interesting.

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:48.700
Like, you know, like named, named entity recognition used to be like this really challenging thing.

00:44:48.700 --> 00:44:52.541
You have to have a massive tech stack specifically for that.

00:44:52.541 --> 00:44:56.020
And now you just like throw it at any random LLM and it does a good job.

00:44:56.020 --> 00:44:58.780
If I'm a barbarian, what's named entity recognition?

00:44:58.780 --> 00:45:01.700
Like, you know, if you've got like a blog post and you just want to extract all the

00:45:01.700 --> 00:45:04.941
people's names that were mentioned in it, like that's, you can't do that with a regular

00:45:04.941 --> 00:45:05.941
expression.

00:45:05.941 --> 00:45:06.941
One of the hard problems is naming things.

00:45:06.941 --> 00:45:07.941
Yeah.

00:45:07.941 --> 00:45:08.941
Yeah.

00:45:08.941 --> 00:45:09.941
Extracting names from things.

00:45:09.941 --> 00:45:16.220
Like I remember, um, a very long time ago, Hilary Mason had this really cool blog post

00:45:16.220 --> 00:45:22.801
where she scraped like 200 chocolate chip cookie recipes and then created like the average

00:45:22.801 --> 00:45:25.340
chocolate chip cookie recipe, which she then made.

00:45:25.340 --> 00:45:28.020
And like back then that was like, that was a lot of work.

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:30.220
I was like, wow, that is so cool.

00:45:30.220 --> 00:45:32.061
And that is now something I could do today.

00:45:32.061 --> 00:45:36.741
And you know, like 30 minutes with, or less than that, you just ask chat GPT for a chocolate

00:45:36.741 --> 00:45:37.741
cookie recipe.

00:45:37.741 --> 00:45:38.741
And that's basically what you're getting.

00:45:38.741 --> 00:45:39.741
Yeah.

00:45:39.741 --> 00:45:40.741
Yeah.

00:45:40.741 --> 00:45:41.741
Yeah.

00:45:41.741 --> 00:45:45.220
Ben Stancil mentioned something about this too, where like, and having to do with data

00:45:45.220 --> 00:45:50.820
work, like support, if you have like thousands of support tickets, you know, as a data team,

00:45:50.820 --> 00:45:55.621
you might have used to go in and tried to analyze it and maybe like calculate a number

00:45:55.621 --> 00:45:57.621
from support tickets.

00:45:57.621 --> 00:46:03.580
He mentioned, yeah, like nowadays you might even just dump your support tickets into like

00:46:03.580 --> 00:46:08.700
chat GPT or something and just ask like your question directly.

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:11.340
Whereas before we were trying to roll up numbers to try to kind of.

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:12.340
Yeah.

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:13.340
You try to have to do some sort of like.

00:46:13.340 --> 00:46:14.340
In between.

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:15.340
Yeah.

00:46:15.340 --> 00:46:17.981
You have to like take the unstructured thing and then, you know, run some models over it,

00:46:17.981 --> 00:46:22.300
maybe some classifiers, maybe some sentiment analysis, maybe you're like extracting specific

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:24.381
signal from it.

00:46:24.381 --> 00:46:28.460
And then you get this like very, very limited structured representation of it, topic, you

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:30.580
know, topic clustering or whatever.

00:46:30.580 --> 00:46:34.981
And then you like try to figure that out, figure out what's going on based on that.

00:46:34.981 --> 00:46:41.861
And some of that actually is still very complimentary, I would say, because, you know, LLMs are non-deterministic

00:46:41.861 --> 00:46:45.101
and they're not very precise, especially for things that are like if you're just dumping

00:46:45.101 --> 00:46:49.861
a bunch of unstructured data into them for things that are require, you know, numerical

00:46:49.861 --> 00:46:54.700
calculations and things like marrying those two things can be challenging.

00:46:54.700 --> 00:47:00.621
But for things like, you know, what are the most common, you know, user complaints?

00:47:00.621 --> 00:47:04.180
I've done this in Hex on Hex data.

00:47:04.180 --> 00:47:07.420
And you can it's actually like very, very fast and very powerful to pull out that kind

00:47:07.420 --> 00:47:08.420
of signal.

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:09.420
Yeah.

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:10.420
Are you saying like user complaints?

00:47:10.420 --> 00:47:14.381
You can get surprisingly far by putting a lot of the feedback in and just asking.

00:47:14.381 --> 00:47:15.381
Yeah.

00:47:15.381 --> 00:47:16.381
Yeah.

00:47:16.381 --> 00:47:17.700
Just get straight to the kind of where you're going.

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:18.700
Yeah.

00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:20.781
And you probably want to move into the more structured realm when you're getting to the

00:47:20.781 --> 00:47:25.340
point where you're like trying to be like, OK, well, what fraction of users are asking

00:47:25.340 --> 00:47:26.340
about this?

00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:30.501
And, you know, you know, how does that compare to like time to resolve the tickets and things

00:47:30.501 --> 00:47:31.501
like that?

00:47:31.501 --> 00:47:34.781
Then you probably do want to start to shift into like a more classically structured data.

00:47:34.781 --> 00:47:37.861
Often you just want like imagine like someone with all the support tickets and they're just

00:47:37.861 --> 00:47:41.661
going to give you the vibes like that's super useful, even if they can't tell you, like

00:47:41.661 --> 00:47:44.381
25% support tickets are about blah.

00:47:44.381 --> 00:47:45.381
Yeah.

00:47:45.381 --> 00:47:46.381
Yeah.

00:47:46.381 --> 00:47:49.501
I like the alternative data science workflow.

00:47:49.501 --> 00:47:53.501
If you have this like from like R for data science, you have this workflow that's like

00:47:53.501 --> 00:47:56.460
import, tidy, transform.

00:47:56.460 --> 00:47:58.501
I like the alternative, which is like copy paste.

00:47:58.501 --> 00:47:59.501
Yeah.

00:47:59.501 --> 00:48:00.501
Just try to get to.

00:48:00.501 --> 00:48:02.261
Just give me the vibes and then you like.

00:48:02.261 --> 00:48:03.261
And then you can iterate.

00:48:03.261 --> 00:48:07.460
But I think I even find that with like some of my projects where I'm just like, hey, Claude,

00:48:07.460 --> 00:48:08.820
just Claude Code, just do this.

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:12.981
Like look at this transcript, do the stuff to it.

00:48:12.981 --> 00:48:15.940
And then I'm like, OK, this this it feels like this has teeth.

00:48:16.501 --> 00:48:20.141
And then I'm like, OK, now Claude Code, write the code that will do that for me, like reliably

00:48:20.141 --> 00:48:21.141
and reproducibly.

00:48:21.141 --> 00:48:25.540
Like that's such a just to be able to do that very, very quick initial prototype to say

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:29.300
like, is there anything here that I should bother exploring further?

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:32.381
You have come from, you know, co-founder, your CTO.

00:48:32.381 --> 00:48:33.381
I'm really curious.

00:48:33.381 --> 00:48:37.540
Like what does success look for you look like for you, especially, you know, in the age

00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:38.540
of AI?

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:40.981
Maybe how has that changed throughout your career?

00:48:41.501 --> 00:48:49.940
Yeah, I think starting a company has really forced me to like disentangle myself from

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:53.460
classic indicators of success, if that makes sense.

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:54.460
And there's a couple of reasons for that.

00:48:54.460 --> 00:48:56.861
One is you'll just drive yourself absolutely insane.

00:48:56.861 --> 00:49:00.700
You're like that company has how much revenue and they raised how much like you just like

00:49:00.700 --> 00:49:02.741
you just can't compare yourself.

00:49:02.741 --> 00:49:04.940
Like a lot of us, you know, come up through school.

00:49:04.940 --> 00:49:10.261
You're basically like ruthlessly, you know, trained to optimize this like one metric and

00:49:10.261 --> 00:49:12.861
like compare yourself against all the other people to get into.

00:49:12.861 --> 00:49:15.861
And like you just have to just let all of that go.

00:49:15.861 --> 00:49:22.761
Like you just have to let the numbers go and focus on, you know, what you can control.

00:49:22.761 --> 00:49:27.101
And I think the other thing about being a co-founder that I found, I found that the

00:49:27.101 --> 00:49:33.901
hardest part about being a co-founder is that you are, you know, the job changes every six

00:49:33.901 --> 00:49:35.621
months.

00:49:35.621 --> 00:49:40.241
And so what that means is just as you're getting your feet under you, you have to do something

00:49:40.241 --> 00:49:41.401
different.

00:49:41.401 --> 00:49:44.281
And so you're always in this mode of like, I'm not good at the thing I'm supposed to

00:49:44.281 --> 00:49:45.281
be doing right now.

00:49:45.281 --> 00:49:46.560
I'm learning how to do this.

00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:50.720
And for someone who, you know, generally, you know, had a relatively successful career

00:49:50.720 --> 00:49:55.241
prior to that, you're like, you're used to operating in areas where you're good and you're

00:49:55.241 --> 00:49:58.080
strong and things that you were hard for and you interviewed well for and you're like great

00:49:58.080 --> 00:50:01.601
at software engineering and being a co-founder is just like not like that.

00:50:01.601 --> 00:50:07.320
And so all of these like classic ways of being like I am successful just kind of go out the

00:50:07.320 --> 00:50:08.840
window.

00:50:08.840 --> 00:50:12.161
And I think for me, I just kind of keep coming back to a couple of different things.

00:50:12.161 --> 00:50:16.040
One is, are we building something that, you know, is doing good for the world?

00:50:16.040 --> 00:50:19.080
That is a useful contribution.

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:21.960
And then am I enjoying what I'm doing?

00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:23.560
Is this a good use of my time?

00:50:23.560 --> 00:50:26.720
And as long as I feel like and, you know, you don't have to enjoy everything that you're

00:50:26.720 --> 00:50:28.121
doing, but like sort of in the macro.

00:50:28.121 --> 00:50:32.121
And as long as you feel like those two things are happening, then I think that's like a

00:50:32.121 --> 00:50:36.121
pretty it's actually like it's quite rare to really be nailing both of those things.

00:50:36.121 --> 00:50:40.401
And so that's kind of how I've been thinking about my own personal career success.

00:50:40.401 --> 00:50:44.521
Do you feel like there was one pivot that was like this is the hardest thing or this

00:50:44.521 --> 00:50:46.281
is the easiest thing?

00:50:46.281 --> 00:50:52.161
Was there one pivot that felt like it hit harder than the rest?

00:50:52.161 --> 00:50:53.161
I don't know.

00:50:53.161 --> 00:50:59.320
I think in some ways I kind of from the beginning of Hex had to make that pivot even just to

00:50:59.320 --> 00:51:02.401
start the company.

00:51:02.401 --> 00:51:09.440
And it was a bit of a sort of mental hurdle to get over because prior to that, my career

00:51:09.440 --> 00:51:10.960
had been extremely linear.

00:51:10.960 --> 00:51:15.641
It was like I see and then manager and then manager of managers.

00:51:15.641 --> 00:51:21.161
And then like, you know, you just kind of like walking up that and taking a step away

00:51:21.161 --> 00:51:22.161
from that.

00:51:22.161 --> 00:51:27.241
And and also candidly, like, you know, I went into Hex being like, I don't know if this

00:51:27.241 --> 00:51:28.241
is going to work.

00:51:28.241 --> 00:51:29.241
Right.

00:51:29.241 --> 00:51:31.180
You know, most startups don't work.

00:51:31.180 --> 00:51:40.580
We may all be back on the job market in a year or two, and that would feel like failure.

00:51:40.580 --> 00:51:44.861
But I think getting kind of getting past that and being like, you know, is that the worst

00:51:44.861 --> 00:51:45.861
thing in the world?

00:51:45.861 --> 00:51:51.101
Like, we try something and it doesn't work out like maybe you're like slightly embarrassed.

00:51:51.101 --> 00:51:52.101
I don't know.

00:51:52.101 --> 00:51:54.101
Like, what is the actual negative outcome there?

00:51:54.101 --> 00:51:56.340
And kind of pushing through that feeling of like, oh, what if it fails?

00:51:56.340 --> 00:51:58.460
Well, what if it does fail?

00:51:58.460 --> 00:51:59.460
So what?

00:51:59.460 --> 00:52:00.460
We all go like we're all software engineers.

00:52:00.741 --> 00:52:05.700
And I think that was probably the biggest mindset change and even just jumping off and

00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:11.460
then, you know, having to kind of repeatedly remind myself of that over the years.

00:52:11.460 --> 00:52:16.580
One of the frameworks I found really useful is like this idea of maximizing versus satisficing.

00:52:16.580 --> 00:52:21.381
Like, I think I feel like, you know, data people, people have had good grades at school,

00:52:21.381 --> 00:52:25.661
like your natural thing is to always like find the best, like maximize.

00:52:25.661 --> 00:52:29.781
Like I find this a lot when I'm trying to like buy a hotel somewhere, like I do so much

00:52:29.781 --> 00:52:33.940
comparison shopping, because I'm trying to find like the best combination of location

00:52:33.940 --> 00:52:34.940
and price.

00:52:34.940 --> 00:52:35.940
Hotel medicine.

00:52:35.940 --> 00:52:36.940
Exactly.

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:42.021
Whereas the alternative approach of like satisficing of just finding the first thing that's going

00:52:42.021 --> 00:52:43.021
to be like good enough.

00:52:43.021 --> 00:52:46.340
Like, that's such a powerful like mindset change.

00:52:46.340 --> 00:52:51.320
Like you don't have to be like, not everything in your life has to be the best.

00:52:51.320 --> 00:52:55.141
Just like finding the things that like make you where you'll be like happy and satisfied.

00:52:55.540 --> 00:53:02.501
There's an interesting story of like the, you know, step one to two feels hard, but

00:53:02.501 --> 00:53:06.060
what was way harder was step zero to one.

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:11.060
And you know, what, what mindset shifts do you have to do to even get to a spot where

00:53:11.060 --> 00:53:14.580
you're at zero versus, you know, continuing to just go like, oh, well, I'm at one, I can

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:16.220
go to two, I can go to three.

00:53:16.220 --> 00:53:19.940
It's like the same with like growing a team, like the first person, like going from zero

00:53:19.940 --> 00:53:21.741
to one is really hard.

00:53:21.741 --> 00:53:23.101
And one to two, that's not so bad.

00:53:23.101 --> 00:53:27.220
And then it just gets kind of get on the downhill slope a little bit.

00:53:27.220 --> 00:53:28.220
Yeah.

00:53:28.220 --> 00:53:37.621
This is a pretty common piece of coaching that I give to engineers, which is, it's basically

00:53:37.621 --> 00:53:41.741
telling them, like giving them permission not to be miserable.

00:53:41.741 --> 00:53:42.741
I love that.

00:53:42.741 --> 00:53:43.741
Yeah.

00:53:43.741 --> 00:53:46.781
They, they, they often, sometimes in engineering, sometimes again, sometimes you got to do what

00:53:46.781 --> 00:53:47.781
you got to do.

00:53:48.180 --> 00:53:53.621
You know, sometimes someone like we had an engineer who moved into an engineering manager

00:53:53.621 --> 00:53:56.381
role and was miserable.

00:53:56.381 --> 00:53:58.700
They hated it.

00:53:58.700 --> 00:54:01.420
And I was like, you can go back.

00:54:01.420 --> 00:54:04.621
Like we actually intentionally have set up our work structure such that like senior engineer

00:54:04.621 --> 00:54:06.741
and engineering manager are the same, no pay difference.

00:54:06.741 --> 00:54:11.220
They're just the same level, just a different title, different role.

00:54:11.220 --> 00:54:19.861
And coaching people through their first moment of, I tried it and I didn't like it.

00:54:19.861 --> 00:54:26.180
And I didn't like make it all the way to something that feels like a success moment, but that's

00:54:26.180 --> 00:54:27.180
okay.

00:54:27.180 --> 00:54:29.180
I don't have to get there.

00:54:29.180 --> 00:54:34.661
If I'm really unhappy with what I'm doing, I can just stop.

00:54:34.661 --> 00:54:39.420
And that, especially like we've been talking about for people, high achievers that can

00:54:39.420 --> 00:54:42.940
feel like a failure because sometimes you're in a, you're in a spot where you're actually

00:54:42.940 --> 00:54:45.580
not doing a great job at the thing that you're supposed to be doing.

00:54:45.580 --> 00:54:51.220
And you feel like you have to push through to get to a point where like you've made it,

00:54:51.220 --> 00:54:52.220
but I don't know.

00:54:52.220 --> 00:54:53.820
We only, you only live once, right?

00:54:53.820 --> 00:54:56.340
Like, you know what you do with your time matters.

00:54:56.340 --> 00:55:00.621
And there's only a certain amount of like pushing through.

00:55:00.621 --> 00:55:04.861
And it really depends on like what the underlying reason is that you're not happy.

00:55:04.861 --> 00:55:07.940
There's like lots of different reasons, but usually I see this in a case where someone

00:55:07.940 --> 00:55:10.540
just like, they don't like the role.

00:55:10.540 --> 00:55:11.700
And that's not a failure.

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:14.781
That's just like, you would rather be doing something different.

00:55:14.781 --> 00:55:17.781
Obviously like there's differences where, you know, you just have, sometimes you do

00:55:17.781 --> 00:55:20.460
have to power through like tough times in the company and you just have to like make

00:55:20.460 --> 00:55:21.460
it out the other side.

00:55:21.460 --> 00:55:22.981
But those are temporary things.

00:55:22.981 --> 00:55:24.420
Like the role doesn't change.

00:55:24.420 --> 00:55:25.420
The role is still the role.

00:55:25.420 --> 00:55:27.381
And so if you don't like it, don't do it.

00:55:27.381 --> 00:55:33.101
You mentioned like the kind of uncertainty of starting a startup reflected in this person's

00:55:33.101 --> 00:55:35.180
shift to engineering manager.

00:55:35.861 --> 00:55:41.981
You mentioned, you know, the fear of starting a startup and thinking like, what if it fails?

00:55:41.981 --> 00:55:44.460
And then being able to say like, so what?

00:55:44.460 --> 00:55:47.861
Like I tried it and I'll still have other things to work on.

00:55:47.861 --> 00:55:48.861
I'll have learned and grow.

00:55:48.861 --> 00:55:54.781
I think that like mindset shift between like this experiment failed versus I'm a failure.

00:55:54.781 --> 00:55:57.621
Like that's a really, really big difference.

00:55:57.621 --> 00:56:02.180
And to coach an engineer on that, that like jumping from to being an engineering manager

00:56:02.180 --> 00:56:05.101
is a similar like experiment as starting a company.

00:56:05.101 --> 00:56:07.180
Does it make sense for that person?

00:56:07.180 --> 00:56:13.381
Maybe it is probably as big and as scary of a jump I'd imagine to try this like new way

00:56:13.381 --> 00:56:18.180
of operating and that you might not know how you'll do.

00:56:18.180 --> 00:56:26.420
So it seems like honestly, so it has got to be so relieving or helpful to know like it's

00:56:26.420 --> 00:56:28.981
an experiment and you can switch back.

00:56:28.981 --> 00:56:29.981
Yeah.

00:56:29.981 --> 00:56:32.781
And now that we've had, we've had this happen in various parts of the organization a couple

00:56:32.781 --> 00:56:33.781
of times now.

00:56:33.781 --> 00:56:37.861
And again, it creates this permission structure for people to just try things.

00:56:37.861 --> 00:56:42.300
And that obviously has like huge positive externalities when people are like less afraid

00:56:42.300 --> 00:56:44.021
to go out on a limb and try something new.

00:56:44.021 --> 00:56:47.700
I mean, again, being the first person to go from being an engineering manager back to

00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:49.420
senior engineer, it's like super scary.

00:56:49.420 --> 00:56:52.021
Like if you're the fifth one, like, yeah, that's a good point.

00:56:52.021 --> 00:56:54.261
It kind of like snowballs, I guess.

00:56:54.261 --> 00:56:55.261
Yeah.

00:56:55.261 --> 00:56:59.940
It does feel like right now, like being, like creating an engineering culture where people

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:03.580
are excited to try experiments, even knowing they're not going to succeed.

00:57:03.580 --> 00:57:08.381
Like that's like particularly today with AI, just so, so important.

00:57:08.381 --> 00:57:09.381
Okay.

00:57:09.381 --> 00:57:13.460
We went through Hex, life as a CTO.

